/**
  @page guides-swift Fitting a mesh interface with SWIFT
  @brief Mathematical model, solver stages, usage, diagnostics and extension points.

  @tableofcontents

  SWIFT denotes the Strain-distributed Welsch Implicit-interface Fitting
  Technique. Robust Welsch fitting drives the marked interface towards the
  target, while centered strain distributes the displacement through the mesh.

  Start with @ref examples-swift-background "the background adaptation and
  fitting tutorial" for a complete runnable MMG–MinSTCut–SWIFT sequence,
  its actual meshes and independently checked circle-fitting error.

  The shared `Reconstruction.cpp` example in `examples/Adaptation/SWIFT` is
  compiled as `SWIFT_ReconstructionP1`, `SWIFT_ReconstructionP2` and
  `SWIFT_ReconstructionP3`. Only the displacement degree changes; named flags,
  lobed circle/sphere targets, diagnostics and XDMF output share one implementation.
  Use `--help` to list controls and `--n=16 --dimension=2 --lobes=4` for a 2D target.
  Calibration uses the reconstruction executables with the same named controls.

  @section guides-swift-setup Setup and ownership

  @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Problem fits marked mesh facets to a supplied zero level
  set. It writes the accumulated displacement into the constructor's trial-function
  solution. Applying that displacement to mesh geometry is an application step.
  The mesh is obtained from the displacement finite element space.

  The mesh, finite element space, trial and test functions must outlive the
  solver. Use matching vector-valued trial and test spaces on the local Eigen
  backend. Mark the interface facets and prepare the mesh connectivity before
  solving. The target gradient must be the derivative of the target level set
  at the displaced points; no target Hessian is required.

  @ref Rodin::Geometry::MinSTCut is the canonical classifier used by the
  reconstruction examples. It balances cell-average phase fidelity against
  the measure of separating facets. Apply its @c inside and @c outside sets
  to cell attributes and its @c cut set to interface facets before fitting.
  Classification prepares the interface labels before the SWIFT optimization.

  @code{.cpp}
  // mesh already has its interface facets marked with Interface.
  P1<Math::SpatialVector<Real>, Geometry::LocalMesh> space(mesh, 2);
  // u owns the accumulated displacement; v tests increments on the same space.
  TrialFunction u(space);
  TestFunction v(space);
  Adaptation::SWIFT::Problem fitting(u, v);
  Adaptation::SWIFT::Parameters parameters;
  // The reference spacing stays fixed as the displacement changes.
  parameters.model.h = referenceSpacing;
  fitting.setParameters(parameters).setInterfaceAttribute(Interface);
  // Zero increments keep this boundary at its current position.
  fitting += DirichletBC(u, VectorFunction(Real(0), Real(0))).on(FixedBoundary);

  // Fit to a vertical line using its exact gradient; no Hessian is needed.
  RealFunction phi([](const Geometry::Point& point) {
    return point.x() - Real(0.55);
  });
  VectorFunction gradient(Real(1), Real(0));
  const auto report = fitting.solve(phi, gradient);
  // Check report.reason, geometricSup, minJ and maxQRel before using the result.
  // Applying displacement to mesh geometry is a separate application step.
  const auto& displacement = u.getSolution();
  @endcode

  Homogeneous boundary conditions constrain each increment to zero, holding the
  current boundary position fixed.
  Nonzero increment values and identification conditions are rejected.
  For another solve on the same space, retain the solver and update its
  parameters or target. The existing displacement is the next initial guess.
  Create a new solver when replacing the mesh or finite element space.

  Applying the result is a separate application decision. Vertex displacement
  is appropriate for a linear geometric mesh. Higher-order geometry is updated through its transformations.

  For an affine simplicial mesh, @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Adapt owns the
  vector P1 space and fitting problem and applies valid displacements in place:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Prepare connectivity and mark interface facets before constructing adapt.
  Adaptation::SWIFT::Adapt adapt(mesh);
  Adaptation::SWIFT::Parameters controls;
  controls.model.h = referenceSpacing; // Keep the background scale fixed.
  adapt.setParameters(controls).setInterfaceAttribute(Interface);
  // Use the owned trial function for additional boundary conditions.
  auto& trial = adapt.getTrialFunction();
  adapt.getProblem() +=
    DirichletBC(trial, VectorFunction(Real(0), Real(0))).on(FixedBoundary);
  const auto adapted = adapt.execute(phi, gradient); // Mesh is updated here.
  @endcode

  Each execute call resets displacement to zero on the current mesh. It applies
  a result when `qualityBudgetSatisfied` is true, including valid best-effort
  exits, and leaves the mesh untouched otherwise. The report describes the
  fit relative to that call's starting mesh. The mesh must outlive the workflow;
  replacing connectivity requires a new workflow. Classification and remeshing
  remain application responsibilities. `getProblem()` provides the same metric,
  boundary-condition and monitor interfaces as an explicitly constructed problem.

  | Entry point | Context/backend | Status |
  |-------------|-----------------|--------|
  | `SWIFT::Problem(u, v)` | Supplied local Eigen trial/test storage | Supported, including higher-order spaces |
  | `SWIFT::Adapt(mesh)` | Local mesh, Eigen P1 storage | Supported for affine simplicial geometry |
  | `SWIFT::Problem(u, v)` | PETSc trial/test storage | Explicitly unsupported |
  | `SWIFT::Adapt(mesh)` | MPI mesh, requiring PETSc | Construction disabled pending distributed implementation |

  Storage and mesh context determine backend selection.
  An MPI mesh will use PETSc when that specialization is implemented; it never
  falls back to Eigen. Distributed assembly requires the corresponding backend implementation.

  @section guides-swift-model Mathematical model

  The displacement metric is @f$M=F+D@f$: fitting observes normal motion on
  the marked interface, while distribution couples that motion to the volume.
  The metric defines the quadratic problem for each displacement increment. Quality is controlled by affine squared hinges on the increment and nonlinear
  checks on the accepted mesh.

  For the current map @f$T_k(x)=x+u_k(x)@f$, set
  @f$A_k=I+\nabla u_k@f$, @f$j_k=\det A_k@f$ and
  @f$g_k=\nabla\phi(T_k(x))@f$. The two bilinear forms are
  @f[
    F_k[v,z]=\kappa_F G^{-2}\int_{\Gamma_0}
      (g_k\cdot v)(g_k\cdot z)\,dS_x,
    \qquad
  D_k[v,z]=h\int_{\Omega_k}\left[
    \kappa_{\rm dev}(\operatorname{dev}\varepsilon(v)
         -\overline{\operatorname{dev}\varepsilon(v)}):
        (\operatorname{dev}\varepsilon(z)
         -\overline{\operatorname{dev}\varepsilon(z)})
    +\frac{\kappa_{\rm div}}{d}
        (\operatorname{div}v-\overline{\operatorname{div}v})
        (\operatorname{div}z-\overline{\operatorname{div}z})
    \right]dy.
  @f]
  Here @f$\Gamma_0@f$ and @f$\Omega_0@f$ are the reference interface and volume;
  @f$G@f$ is a fixed sampled target-gradient normalization. Fitting uses
  squared-residual curvature without the level-set Hessian or robust weights.
  The force and outer Armijo merit instead come from the fixed-scale Welsch
  objective
  @f[
    E(u)=G^{-2}\int_{\Gamma_0}\frac{\sigma^2}{2}
        \left(1-e^{-\phi(x+u(x))^2/\sigma^2}\right)\,dS_x,
    \qquad f_k=-DE(u_k).
  @f]
  The reference measure and robust scale @f$\sigma@f$ are held fixed during a
  solve. The quadratic metric preconditions descent of the robust fitting objective.

  Directional Newton scales the predictor before the affine hinge solve:
  @f$\bar p_k=a_kp_k@f$ and @f$\bar M_k=M_k/a_k@f$. The inner model is
  @f[
    \min_{v\in V_h}\left\{
       \tfrac12\bar M_k[v,v]-f_k[v]+B_k(v)\right\},
  @f]
  where @f$B_k@f$ is the squared-hinge penalty on frozen Jacobian and distortion
  slacks. The penalty and actual-quality checks use the same cached closed-form
  reference covering. The generating lattice is shifted and contracted while preserving its point count.
  The resulting reference covering determines the quality witnesses; continuous
  quality between witnesses requires further analysis.
  Separate positive Jacobian and
  distortion weights mix equal mass with normalized nonlinear guard penetration
  at the current and full-predictor geometries. Each family preserves mapped
  cell mass, and its weights stay fixed during the inner solve. The equal
  fraction is one half and dimensionless risks are capped at 100. Inverted
  predictor points receive maximal distortion risk without evaluating distortion.
  Validation checks every witness without weights. Equal reference weights, normalized to reference volume and mapped to each cell,
  determine cell mass. Adaptive penalty weights are assigned within that mass.
  Coverings sample nonlinear quality, while integration rules provide polynomial
  exactness.
  Its stationarity residual controls inner convergence. The outer
  Armijo search then decreases @f$E@f$ while enforcing the actual sampled
  quality budget. See the @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Problem "SWIFT class reference"
  for the slack definitions, penalty scaling, Newton system, line search and
  geometric stopping equations.

  For current-coordinate fields, @f$\varepsilon(v)=\operatorname{sym}\nabla_yv@f$;
  the overbars are averages over the entire current domain @f$\Omega_k@f$.
  Both distribution coefficients are independent.

  @section guides-swift-stages What the solver does

  Each outer iteration has four stages:

  1. Assemble the robust fitting force and the metric @f$M=F+D@f$.
     Fitting makes motion responsive to the target normal. Distribution couples
     neighboring elements through variation of current strain about its global
     mean. Coherent affine motions are free, including shear and anisotropic
     stretching. Spatial strain variation is resisted, and shared DOFs transmit compatible motion
  between elements.
  2. Solve for an unconstrained predictor. Directional Newton rescales the
     physical predictor and its quadratic metric before applying the hinges.
  3. Use native @ref Rodin::Solver::NewtonSolver to solve the frozen quadratic
     model with affine quadratic quality hinges. Inactive hinges need no Newton
     correction. Inner convergence uses the stationarity residual.
  4. Backtrack with Armijo on the robust fitting energy. Every trial must also
     satisfy the actual sampled Jacobian and distortion limits. Accept the
     displacement only after both checks pass.

  The retained linear adapter supports CG, SparseLU and optional MUMPS. MUMPS
  retains symbolic analysis when the sparsity pattern is unchanged. Numerical
  factors are reused only when the assembled operator is unchanged. Global
  centering is represented by a low-rank subtraction from the sparse core.
  Direct solvers factor the equivalent augmented system; CG applies the
  centered operator without a dense matrix. No gauge, mass penalty or
  stabilization is added. Singular systems remain subject to the selected
  backend. With both centered weights positive, distribution is coercive in
  H1 modulo global affine motions on a fixed connected bounded Lipschitz domain.
  The mesh-scale factor and changing domain preclude an unconditional uniform bound.

  @section guides-swift-curved Higher-order fitting and curved geometry

  The shared reconstruction program uses a displacement space of degree
  @f$p\in\{1,2,3\}@f$ on the same classified simplicial mesh. Trial and test
  functions define the fitting problem directly:

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/Reconstruction.cpp swift-problem

  After solving, the displacement is still a field on the reference mesh.
  Construct a separate output mesh and evaluate that field at every geometry
  node while retaining the reference geometry for evaluation. The program
  installs new transformations on every positive-dimensional entity:

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/Reconstruction.cpp swift-geometry

  Updating the corner vertices alone would discard the higher-order geometric
  information. Keeping the reference mesh unchanged also prevents later
  displacement evaluations from using already displaced coordinates.

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages rodin-mesh-stages-pair}

  @parblock

  @image html SWIFTCurvedFitting-background.png "Reference classification."
  @image html SWIFTCurvedFitting-moved.png "Actual quadratic fitted mesh."

  @endparblock

  Cyan identifies the inside cells; the navy curve is the analytic zero level
  set of the four-lobe target.

  The illustrated run uses 16 grid points per axis, 450 triangles and the
  quadratic executable with its default model and classification settings:

  @code{.sh}
  SWIFT_ReconstructionP2 --n=16 --dimension=2 --lobes=4 \
    --linear-solver=sparse-lu --output=lobed
  @endcode

  Its target in polar coordinates about @f$(0.5,0.5)@f$ is
  @f$\phi=r-0.25-0.05\cos(4\theta)@f$. The checked local run reached the
  sampled geometric target in two outer steps and five inner corrections:

  | Response | Measured value |
  | :--- | ---: |
  | Sampled maximum normalized residual | @f$1.87068\times10^{-4}@f$ |
  | Automatic target, @f$(1/15)^3@f$ | @f$2.96296\times10^{-4}@f$ |
  | Minimum sampled relative Jacobian | @f$0.25846@f$ |
  | Maximum sampled relative distortion | @f$3.61738@f$ |

  Unlike the fixed-box background tutorial, this program leaves the exterior
  box unconstrained. Its exterior therefore moves with the displacement.
  These are single-run sampled diagnostics. The lobed level-set residual is
  normalized by the target gradient and estimates local geometric distance;
  a refinement study and independent geometric checks are needed to measure
  approximation order and behavior between quality witnesses.

  @section guides-swift-controls Main controls

  Model coefficients are grouped under `parameters.model`. Accuracy settings
  and work budgets are independent groups under `parameters.convergence`:
  `tolerance` and `iterations`. Globalization, linear-solver selection and
  quadrature have their own groups. All values in the following table are
  defaults except the required reference size.

  The model defaults follow the current shared leader of the ongoing 2D
  calibration. The defaults are provisional pending completion of the three-dimensional
  calibration. The
  reconstruction examples use dimensionless classifier smoothing
  @f$\widehat s=0.01@f$, supplied to MinSTCut as the physical weight
  @f$h\widehat s@f$; MinSTCut's own default facet function remains one.

  | Parameter | C++ | Description | Value |
  |-----------|-----|-------------|-------|
  | @f$h@f$ | `model.h` | Fixed background reference size | Required |
  | @f$\kappa_F@f$ | `model.fit` | Target-normal motion stiffness | @f$1@f$ |
  | @f$\kappa_{\rm dev}@f$ | `model.distribution.deviatoric` | Cost of deviatoric-strain variation about its global mean | @f$10^{-4}@f$ |
  | @f$\kappa_{\rm div}@f$ | `model.distribution.divergence` | Cost of divergence variation about its global mean, normalized by @f$d@f$ | @f$10^{-4}@f$ |
  | @f$\mu_j@f$ | `model.hinge.jacobian` | Jacobian recovery strength | @f$10@f$ |
  | @f$\mu_Q@f$ | `model.hinge.distortion` | Distortion recovery strength | @f$100@f$ |
  | @f$\gamma@f$ | `model.qualityGuard` | Guard fraction defining hinge activation margins | @f$0.1@f$ |
  | @f$j_{\mathrm{safe}}@f$ | `model.jacobian` | Relative Jacobian floor in the hinge and actual acceptance checks | @f$10^{-2}@f$ |
  | @f$Q_{\max}@f$ | `model.distortion` | Spendable relative-distortion budget | @f$10@f$ |
  | @f$\sigma@f$ | `model.robustScale` | Welsch robustness scale in level-set units | Automatic (`0`) |
  | @f$\lambda_{\max}@f$ | `globalization.maxStepOverH` | Optional predictor-motion limit divided by @f$h@f$; zero leaves directional Newton unrestricted | @f$0@f$ |
  | @f$c_A@f$ | `globalization.armijo` | Armijo sufficient-decrease coefficient | @f$10^{-4}@f$ |
  | @f$\varepsilon_{\mathrm{geom}}@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.geometric` | Maximum sampled normalized-residual target | @f$h^{p+1}@f$, automatic (`0`) |
  | @f$\tau_{\mathrm{rel}}@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.innerRelative` | Inner stationarity tolerance relative to fitting force | @f$10^{-3}@f$ |
  | @f$\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.innerAbsolute` | Absolute inner stationarity allowance | @f$10^{-12}@f$ |
  | @f$\tau_{\mathrm{lin}}@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.linearRelative` | Relative linear residual tolerance for every backend | @f$10^{-6}@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{outer}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.outer` | Maximum outer iterations | @f$30@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{inner}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.inner` | Maximum Newton corrections per outer iteration | @f$15@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{CG}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.linear` | Iteration cap per CG solve only | @f$1000@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{bt}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.backtracks` | Maximum outer trial halvings | @f$32@f$ |
  | @f$\varepsilon_{\mathrm{step}}/h@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.stepOverH` | Small accepted-motion threshold for a best-effort exit | @f$5\times10^{-4}@f$ |
  | @f$\varepsilon_E@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.energy` | Relative energy-change threshold for a best-effort exit | @f$10^{-8}@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{stag}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.stagnation` | Consecutive small steps or energy changes before stagnation exit | @f$5@f$ |
  | @f$q@f$ | `quadrature.order` | Common integration override | Automatic (`0`) |
  | @f$q_\Gamma@f$ | `quadrature.surface` | Surface energy, force and fitting integration; overrides the common order | Automatic (`0`) |
  | @f$q_\Omega@f$ | `quadrature.volume` | Distribution integration; overrides the common order | Automatic (`0`) |
  | @f$m_{j,Q}@f$ | `sampling.subdivision` | Generating lattice subdivisions for the shared hinge/quality covering | Automatic (`0`) |
  | @f$q_{\mathrm{geom}}@f$ | `quadrature.validation` | Independent geometric-response sampling plus facet vertices | Automatic (`0`) |

  MUMPS is the default when compiled in; otherwise SparseLU is selected.
  Set `linear.solver` and `linear.threads` explicitly for reproducible runs.
  The automatic sampling policies are:

  | Entity and displacement | Surface integration | Volume integration | Quality sampling | Geometric sampling |
  |-------------------------|---------------------|--------------------|------------------|--------------------|
  | Affine simplicial @f$P_1@f$ | @f$8@f$ | @f$2@f$ | @f$2@f$ covering subdivisions | @f$32@f$ + facet vertices |
  | Curved or @f$P_2/P_3@f$ | @f$12@f$ | @f$8@f$ | @f$16@f$ covering subdivisions | @f$32@f$ + facet vertices |

  The affine @f$P_1@f$ policy is calibrated on the tested cases. Higher-order
  policies are provisional and require refinement checks, particularly when
  hinges are active. Orders grow for degrees beyond this table; those floors provide a heuristic starting point for nonlinear integration, to be
  assessed through refinement.
  Selection is per entity. Nonsimplicial volume elements use the higher-order
  volume and quality policies even at degree one; surface integration uses
  the facet's geometry and trace degree.
  Quadrature orders are selected automatically unless overridden by
  `quadrature.order` (example flag `--quadrature-order`). A known fitting tensor on
  an affine entity uses the complete integrand's native order propagation.
  A globally constant target gradient is unchanged by point location and
  yields order @f$2p@f$ for degree-@f$p@f$ displacement functions.
  Elementwise target orders alone cannot be propagated through deformation:
  a moved facet may cross target cells, and curved surface measures may be
  nonpolynomial. Such tensors retain the nonpolynomial interface policy,
  as do the Welsch force and energy; these two use the same integration rule.
  `quadrature.validation` (`--quadrature-validation`) independently
  controls geometric-response sampling separately from `--quadrature-order`.
  The surface and volume settings use `--quadrature-surface` and
  `--quadrature-volume`. Independent quality checks use
  `--sampling-subdivision`, which specifies generating-lattice subdivisions. Tensor cells use Cartesian grids, wedges use triangular
  grids times segments, and pyramids use shrinking square layers.
  Default triangle/tetrahedron counts are @f$6/10@f$ for affine @f$P_1@f$ and
  @f$153/969@f$ for @f$P_2/P_3@f$. Cell vertices are always checked, and common
  quality sampling is configured independently of integration overrides.
  On curved cells, these checks establish quality at the sampled points.
  Example flags follow the C++ parameter hierarchy: dots become hyphens and
  camel-case words are separated by hyphens. Thus `sampling.subdivision`
  maps to `--sampling-subdivision`. Model flags include `--model-fit`,
  `--model-distribution-deviatoric`, `--model-distribution-divergence`,
  `--model-hinge-jacobian`, `--model-hinge-distortion`, `--model-jacobian` and `--model-distortion`.
  Iteration flags are `--convergence-iterations-outer` and `--convergence-iterations-inner`.
  Linear settings use `--linear-solver`, `--linear-threads` and
  `--convergence-tolerance-linear-relative`. Legacy spellings are rejected.

  @section guides-swift-results Reading results

  `report.reason` identifies the termination condition. A geometric target hit is
  `SWIFT::Report::Reason::GeometricTarget`. Budget, small-step and small-energy exits return the current iterate with their
  respective termination reason. The stable text
  token is available through `report.getReasonString()`.

  The main geometric response is `geometricSup`, the maximum sampled normalized
  level-set residual on the complete interface, including its vertices.
  Normalization divides the absolute level-set value by the local target-gradient
  magnitude to give a local distance estimate.
  The automatic target is @f$h^{p+1}@f$, using the reference spacing and the
  interface finite element order. `geometricSup` is a sampled residual estimate. Hausdorff bounds require additional
  geometric analysis, and convergence order is measured over a refinement
  sequence.

  The report's `geometricConstant` is the dimensionless sampled score
  @f[
    C = \frac{D_\infty}{h_0^{p+1}}.
  @f]
  The background reference size @f$h_0@f$ remains fixed throughout fitting.
  This normalization is independent of a user-specified stopping tolerance.
  Geometry traces and canonical example responses expose the score as `geom_c`.

  Monitor `energy`, `geometricSup`, `geometricConstant`, `geometricSupTarget`, `minJ`, `maxQRel`,
  `iterations`, `innerIterations`, `lastInnerIterations`, `maxInnerIterations`
  and `innerRelativeResidual`. The report's `innerIterations` is the total
  correction count, whereas `parameters.convergence.iterations.inner` is the
  per-outer-iteration cap.
  `interfaceMeasure` is the reference integration measure used by the fitting
  energy. Energy and maximum error answer different questions; neither should
  silently replace the other as a stopping certificate.

  The quality budget permits distortion up to its configured limit.
  `qualityBudgetSatisfied` records whether the sampled Jacobian and distortion
  checks satisfy that budget. Inspect the reason and quality fields even
  when the target was not reached.

  @code{.cpp}
  fitting.setMonitor([](const Adaptation::SWIFT::Report& report) {
    // Record or display report.energy, report.geometricSup and quality fields.
  });
  @endcode

  The monitor receives one snapshot per accepted step and one final report,
  including early returns. The final snapshot may repeat the last accepted
  step. The callback is observational and must complete without modifying the problem or
  throwing an exception. `getReport()` holds the last
  completed solve. `trace` additionally enables the examples' diagnostic logs.

  @section guides-swift-extension Adding metric terms

  `getMetric()` exposes an additional native bilinear form that augments the fitting
  and distribution metric.
  Both local and global bilinear integrators use Rodin's normal form interface.

  @code{.cpp}
  fitting.getMetric() += Integral(Dot(u, v));
  fitting.getMetric() -= Integral(Dot(u, v));
  @endcode

  Added terms are assembled again at each outer iteration. They augment the quadratic direction model; the robust energy and fitting force
  retain their definitions.
  Maintain symmetry, nonnegative curvature and compatible unresolved modes.
  The fitting force is derived from the robust energy so that the direction model
  remains consistent with Armijo's descent test.

  @section guides-swift-layout Implementation layout

  All method-specific code lives in `Adaptation/SWIFT/`:
  `Adapt.h` owns the P1 mesh workflow; `Problem.h` owns outer orchestration;
  `FittingTensor.h` and
  `FittingForce.h` assemble fitting; `Distribution.h` assembles distribution;
  `Hinge.h`, `HingeMetric.h`, `HingeForce.h` and `HingeProblem.h` define the
  inner model; `LinearSolver.h` adapts native linear solvers.
  `Parameters.h` and `Report.h` are the configuration and diagnostic surfaces.
  Geometry evaluation uses the shared adaptation deformation infrastructure.
*/
